{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Robo Clash Arena V1 is done and playable end to end in Studio (place 99842799688877). Play is stopped and every debug hook is removed. I haven't saved, published, or changed privacy; Codex still needs to do File > Save to Roblox.\n\n**What's implemented**\n- **Flow:** Title → Robot Select → Mode Select → 1v1 → Results.\n  - Robot Select offers three robots (BOLT, CRUSHER, LANCER), each with its own guns, bombs, pods, legs and dash attack.\n  - Mode Select offers Matchmaking, Battle a Bot, or Back.\n  - Each match opens with a cube drop-in, then READY 3-2-1 LAUNCH!\n  - A match ends in K.O., TIME UP, DRAW or OPPONENT LEFT, with PERFECT shown for a no-damage K.O.\n  - Results show stats, Rematch and Back to Select.\n- **Combat (all decided on the server):**\n  - The client only sends validated, rate-limited intents.\n  - Homing guns, arcing bombs, slow pods.\n  - Endurance → DOWN (3 pink pips) → REBIRTH.\n  - Dash attacks, pits, knockback.\n  - 180 s timer; on time-out the higher HP% wins.\n- **Matches:** the match code supports any number of players, so FFA can come later.\n  - Matchmaking is an in-server queue, and every match gets its own private arena.\n  - If an opponent leaves mid-match, the remaining player wins.\n  - A match with no humans left is torn down.\n- **Bot:** the server-side AI keeps range, strafes, dodges, and fires with a reaction delay and aim error.\n- **Look:**\n  - Holo arena in a dark blue void with bloom, and chunky robots with glowing visors.\n  - About 47° auto-zoom camera; the camera-side walls fade out.\n  - Thick HP bars in the bottom corners, pink reticle, HIT plus red DAMAGE numbers, floating P1/P2 tags.\n  - Tilted announcer text on a pink-edged slash band.\n  - Procedural animation, including the KO slam and victory pose.\n- **This session's additions:**\n  - **Sound:** sci-fi effects and a music loop, mostly from the Pro Sound Effects, APM and Roblox libraries, each confirmed to load.\n  - **Touch:** layout pass; on touch devices the HP panels move to the top corners.\n  - **Pacing:** global damage scale of 0.7.\n\n**How I tested** (live Studio playtests, checking warnings and errors on both server and client after each change; all clean)\n- Full flow with all three robots and every weapon, using real keyboard input.\n- Bot to K.O. (51 s against a mostly idle player), TIME UP, Rematch, Back to Select.\n- Matchmaking with a fake opponent, the opponent leaving mid-match, and the human leaving a bot match.\n- DOWN and REBIRTH checked frame by frame on the client.\n- Touch layout screenshotted with a temporary override.\n- All temporary debug code (server test hook, fake opponent, bot idle flag, touch override) is removed, and a final clean run confirmed the hook is gone.\n\n**Known issues and limits**\n- Gamepad and touch input couldn't be truly exercised: the Studio tooling sends gamepad presses as keyboard input and has no touch. The bindings are in place, and touch buttons use the same action path as the keys. Both need a check on a real phone and a real gamepad.\n- Real two-human matchmaking hasn't been tested on a live server.\n- Lock-on always picks the nearest rival, which is fine for 1v1; FFA will need target cycling.\n- The REBIRTH flash blinks fairly slowly (about 0.5 s on, 0.5 s off).\n\n**Docs and git:** I pushed `status.md` and the new `architecture.md` (scripts, remotes, data flow, how to add FFA). I also added playtest and audio lessons to the `roblox-studio-mcp` skill. Game scripts are in `~/src/rca-work/src`, not mirrored into the repo, as AGENTS.md asks. The logs and `prompts/dashboard-config.md` are left uncommitted."}]},"session_id":"cdeac534-366f-4359-ab90-975d2829fcb2"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":4365552,"duration_api_ms":4365552,"is_error":false,"result":"I'll start by reading the repo instructions, skills, and game spec.Let me check the logs folder, since a previous run (\"opus-phase1\") may have already done some work.That log is this session itself, so this is a fresh start. Now connecting to Studio.Luau CLI works. Let me also grab `luau-lsp`, which can analyze code against Roblox API type definitions (catches typos in API names), and test whether Studio can fetch from a local HTTP server.Analyzer works (syntax + lint; unknown globals get flagged). Now testing whether Studio's edit context can fetch from a local HTTP server.Studio blocks requests to local addresses. Studio has a built-in `InstanceFileSyncService` (Script Sync) though; let me check if it can be driven from Luau to link Studio scripts to local files.Sync tool written. Now let me prepare the Studio place: remove leftovers, disable streaming and auto-loading characters, and create the folder/remote structure.Now writing the shared Config module (robot, gun, bomb, pod, legs definitions).Now the shared Util, ProjectileSim (used by both server and client so motion matches), and Mover (movement used by the player client and server bots).Now linking the builders into the sync tree, adding a lint script, and pushing everything to Studio.Requires don't resolve without a sourcemap. I'll have the sync tool also emit a Rojo-style `sourcemap.json` so `luau-lsp` can type-check across modules.Lint is clean. Now the Studio-side loader (applies the bundle to matching script paths, creating folders/scripts as needed).Sync works. Now running the builders in edit mode to create robots, the arena template, the lobby, and lighting.The robots read well (distinct silhouettes and colors), but the statues are sunk into the pedestals and the pedestal glow discs cover the tops. Fixing the lobby builder.I'll put the loader itself in Studio as a tools module so each sync is a one-liner, and a `Rebuild` tools module for the world builders.Robots look distinct and readable. Now checking the arena preview from a high angle.Now `MatchService` (match lifecycle for N participants: arena cloning into slots, intro, launch, timer, KO/time-up/leave, results, rematch voting) and `MatchmakingService` (in-server queue).Now the server entry script that wires remotes (with validation and rate limiting) to the services.Server side is complete and lint-clean. Before writing the client, let me check which built-in particle textures ship with Studio (so effects don't depend on uploaded assets).Built-in particle textures and a few sounds (explosion, jump, land) are available as fallbacks. Now writing the client, starting with shared client state and the UI style kit.Now `RobotAnim`, which poses every robot locally via Motor6D transforms: run cycle, lean, airborne tuck, gun recoil, bomb throw, flinch, lying flat when DOWN, REBIRTH flashing, and the victory pose.Checking that the HUD matches the actual `Style` and `State` helper signatures.Fixing a font-property ordering issue in the HUD and adding the dash-readiness field to `State`. Then I'll re-read the `Mover` API and the server-side intent/start handling before writing `Battle`.Now the server's intent handler, the Start payload, and the events it sends to clients.The search hit the workspace instead of the build folder, so I'll run ripgrep directly.Now the touch controls: a dynamic left-side thumbstick plus big GUN/BOMB/POD/JUMP/DASH buttons, shown only on touch devices or when the debug `ForceTouch` attribute is set.Checking which combatant states the server sets, so the client lock logic matches.Now `Battle`, the in-match client controller: input bindings, local movement, lock-on, the camera, the cube drop-in intro, and the KO sequence.Next is the menu UI. First I'll look up how the lobby statues and pedestals are named, so the select-screen camera can focus on them.Cleaning up two sloppy spots in the Results and Mode screens.Now the client entry script that creates the scaled ScreenGuis, tracks the active input type, and routes server events to the battle controller and the menus.All client modules are written. Running the linter over the whole codebase next.Mostly strict-typing noise, but I'll fix all of it so the lint stays clean.Lint is clean. Checking the rest of `Sound.Play` before pushing everything to Studio.All scripts are in Studio and streaming is off. Before the first playtest I'll confirm the remotes and the pre-built content exist.Everything is in place. Starting the first playtest.The title screen renders correctly. The console output was too large to return, so I'll pull only warnings and errors from the log history.No errors on either side. Clicking PLAY to go to Robot Select.Robot Select looks right. Confirming Bolt and opening Mode Select.The click by coordinates didn't register. I'll check the real viewport size and, as a more reliable approach, give every menu button a stable name so tests can target it by instance path.The viewport is 829×693, matching the screenshot, so the coordinate click probably landed in the wrong place. Instance paths are more reliable; adding stable button names.Coordinate clicks don't reach the GUI in this setup, so I'll stop, sync the named buttons, and drive the menus by instance path.That screen still names the button `Play` explicitly, so I'll use that path.The click target is covered by a core Roblox overlay, most likely the default chat window in the top-left. I'll give the select-screen containers unique names and check the chat setup in Studio.Now the camera fit. For a fixed view direction, I'll solve for the minimum distance that keeps every robot's head and feet inside the frustum, leaving extra room at the bottom for the HUD panels.Pushing the camera change and rebuilding the arena and lighting from the updated builder.The keyboard tool also accepts gamepad buttons, which I can use later. Restarting play and entering a bot match, with screenshots during the cube drop-in.The cubes drop in on the spawns as intended. Checking the READY / 3-2-1 / LAUNCH! sequence and the post-launch framing.Weapons fire and the bot lands hits, but the floating P1/P2 tags and the lock-on reticle aren't visible. Inspecting them on the client.The billboards exist, are enabled, and have the right adornees but don't render, likely because their parent is a plain Folder. Testing live by moving them under a ScreenGui.My idle robot was KO'd in about 25 seconds, and the KO camera ended up inside a part (the whole screen is cyan). Taking another capture to see the state after the KO.The downs stat counts DOWNs a robot *caused*, so I'll relabel the row to match. Also forcing layout-order sorting on every list layout.For the billboards I'll parent them directly to PlayerGui (the setup I just tested live) and track them for cleanup, rather than keeping them in a Folder.Also keeping the KO camera at gameplay pitch (slightly closer) so it can't end up inside walls.The bot's timing logic is reasonable, and an idle target makes it look stronger than it is. I'll judge balance while actually moving. Pushing fixes and starting another match.All the billboard elements are present, enabled, and visible in the hierarchy, so either they don't render or the screen capture doesn't include them. Testing with a minimal red billboard.The red test billboard doesn't show either. Checking whether the billboard is being laid out on screen at all.Replacing the tag and popup builders with screen-space frames.Now wiring the overlay ScreenGui (no safe-area insets, so viewport coordinates map 1:1) in the client entry, and updating world elements after the camera moves each frame.Before restarting: the bot did about 850 damage in 30 seconds while I was moving, which is too punishing. Looking at the bot's timing parameters.Making the bot more forgiving: more aim jitter, weaker homing, slower reactions, and longer gaps