visible even when faded. Checking client logs and sound load status.No warnings or errors, and the music is loaded and playing. Looking at the wall fade code to tone down the near-wall trim.The rays hit nothing, so the slab is either a non-queryable part or a GUI element. Checking GUIs at that screen position.The match has already ended (the bot beat my idle player), so the Results screen is up and the camera is back in the lobby. Taking a fresh capture.The tilted band shows, but it's still visible while the text has already faded. Capturing again to see whether it closes.Pushing the fade fix, then restarting play to test the touch UI.Now enabling the forced-touch override on the client, then starting the bot match.The tilted K.O.! slam on its pink-edged band looks right. One issue: touch controls stay up during the KO sequence. I'll hide them on KO, but first capturing the victory pose.Next is mid-match leave handling. Checking the debug hook and the server's leave path.I'll split the leave logic into a combatant-level drop function. Then a temporary debug command can make the fake PvP opponent \"leave\" through the same code path a real disconnect uses.Pushing, then testing the leave path: matchmaking, a fake opponent, then the opponent leaves mid-fight.The winner gets the arms-up victory pose after the opponent leaves. Checking the results reason and both consoles.Mid-match leave works with no errors on either side. One fix: PERFECT! shouldn't appear when the opponent left. Finding where it's set.Also testing the other direction: the human leaves a bot match, which should tear the match down.The capture missed the down moment because of the lag, so I'll check the rebirth flash numerically: sample the bot's Highlight and local transparency on the client after another knockdown.The client never saw the bot leave Normal. I'll run the knockdown and sample the state attribute in a single server call to see what's happening.The server state machine runs Down for 1.7 s,